# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF IN THIS REPO. THIS FILE IS WHAT ENFORCES IT.
#
# The whole development machine is Windows, so an editor or a tool that writes
# CRLF is not an unusual event here — it is the default failure. What made it
# worth a rule is how the damage shows up, which is nowhere near the cause.
#
# A file committed with CRLF differs from its LF counterpart on EVERY line. Git
# has no way to see a two-line edit inside that; it reports the file as entirely
# rewritten. So a branch that touched two lines of `api/_lib/cards.js` produced a
# 684-line merge conflict, and three files did it at once (PR #740, resolved in
# the integration stack #742). Nothing was wrong with the change. The endings
# were wrong, and the conflict was noise standing between a reviewer and a
# two-line diff.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` fixes it at the only place that holds: git normalizes CRLF
# to LF when it writes the file into the INDEX, whatever the working tree looks
# like. A tool that insists on CRLF locally is then harmless, because what gets
# committed is LF either way. This costs nothing on Windows — git checks the
# file back out however `core.autocrlf` says to.
#
# `scripts/check-line-endings.mjs` is the second layer, and it exists because a
# rule that only lives in configuration is a rule nobody is told they broke: it
# reads the INDEX (not the working tree, which is legitimately CRLF on Windows)
# and fails `npm run lint` on anything that got through. Keep both — this file
# prevents, that one reports.
#
# The repo has no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files, which are the usual reason to carve out a
# CRLF exception. If one is ever added, give it `eol=crlf` here rather than
# weakening the blanket rule.

# Everything git considers text is stored LF, regardless of the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets, marked explicitly rather than left to the content heuristic.
# `text=auto` guesses by looking for NUL bytes, and the guess is wrong for the
# occasional asset that has none — an SVG is XML, so it is text by any heuristic
# and a mangled one is a broken image, not a diff.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# SVG is text and must stay diffable — the logo-art pipeline reads and rewrites
# these, and a reviewer needs to see which path changed.
*.svg   text eol=lf
